From 95acd93765b5d865b0e5640cc73759ad338f6bcb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 06:35:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] parse: a `return`/`break`/`continue` statement keeps its `;`
 (attribute proc macros got `return .. return ..`)

cryptography 48.0.0 src/rust/src/backend/poly1305.rs:124:

    #[pyo3::pymethods]
    impl Poly1305 {
        #[new]
        fn new(key: CffiBuf<'_>) -> CryptographyResult<Poly1305> {
            #[cfg(any(CRYPTOGRAPHY_IS_BORINGSSL, ..))]
            return Ok(Poly1305 { inner: Some(Poly1305Boring::new(key)?) });
            #[cfg(not(any(CRYPTOGRAPHY_IS_LIBRESSL, ..)))]
            return Ok(Poly1305 { inner: Some(Poly1305Open::new(key)?) });
        }
        ..
    }

    poly1305.rs:124-125 error:0: compile_error! "unexpected token, expected `;`"

Parse_ExprBlockLine's flow-control arm (`return`, `break`, `continue`,
`yield`, `do yeet`) consumed the statement's `;` without recording it on
the block line ("don't need silencing": typeck gives a block whose last
statement diverges the type `!` whether or not that statement is the
tail). Fn bodies reach attribute proc macros as text printed from the
AST, and the printer emits a `;` only for lines that recorded one, so
pyo3 got `return Ok(..) #[cfg(..)] return Ok(..)` and syn rejected it.
mrustc's own re-parse accepted that text through the
`#[cfg()] foo #[cfg()] bar` leniency, which is why the printed form was
never noticed.

The `;` is recorded like any other statement's. The HIR block then has
no tail and ends in a diverging statement, which the typeck already
handles (`todo!();` bodies); a tail `return x` without `;` stays a tail.

Assisted-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---

diff --git a/src/parse/expr.cpp b/src/parse/expr.cpp
index 892b378c..160d04bf 100644
--- a/src/parse/expr.cpp
+++ b/src/parse/expr.cpp
@@ -412,7 +412,11 @@ ExprNodeP Parse_ExprBlockLine(TokenStream& lex, bool *add_silence)
                 PUTBACK(tok, lex);
             }
             else {
-                // return/continue/break don't need silencing
+                // The `;` is recorded like any other statement's: typeck gives a block whose last statement diverges the
+                // type `!` whether or not it is the tail, but the AST printer (fn bodies reach attribute proc macros as
+                // re-lexed text) must emit it. `#[cfg(..)] return Ok(..); #[cfg(not(..))] return Ok(..);` (cryptography
+                // 48.0.0 poly1305.rs) went out as `return .. return ..` = syn "unexpected token, expected `;`".
+                *add_silence = true;
             }
             return ret;
             }
